<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="DaPQ" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Questions</title>

  <ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            <term>Histogram Interpretation</term>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p> 
                    Compare your histogram to the shape of a normal distribution. 
                    Does it resemble a bell-curve, or does it show a different pattern. 
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Describe any deviations: Is the distribution bimodal, skewed, flattened, or sharply peaked?
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Based on the shape of your data, would you feel confident using parametric statistics, or would a non-parametric alternative be more appropriate?
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Do you think your sample size was sufficient to make this judgment? 
                    Why or why not?
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            <term>Central Tendency</term>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The mean, median, and mode are all ways to describe the “typical” individual in a population. 
                    Based on your data, do these three statistics differ significantly? 
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    How does the similarity or difference among them relate to the shape of your histogram?
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <term>Variation and Reliability</term>
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        What do the variance, standard deviation, and standard error tell you about your population that the mean alone does not?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Why is it important to report a measure of variation and sample size alongside the mean?
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            <term>Confidence Intervals</term>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Did your calculated 95% confidence interval include the population mean determined by weighing all the beans?
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    What does this tell you about the reliability of your sample mean?
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </li>
  </ol>
            
</section>